From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thelamer <ryankuba@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 09:07:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Encode an NV12 picture in place of copying it

x264 copies every input picture into a frame of its own before it encodes it, interleaving the
chroma of a planar one, while a caller that converts its frames itself can write NV12 where x264
would read it. An 8-bit NV12 picture flagged X264_CSP_IN_PLACE is encoded where it lies: the
input frame's planes point at the caller's for as long as x264 reads them, through the encode it
is passed to and the next one, which takes it as the previous input, and are pointed back at the
frame's own after that (or at x264_encoder_release_input). x264_param_input_layout gives the
buffers that takes, laid out as x264's own frames (stride, offset of the first pixel, size,
aligned to X264_IN_PLACE_ALIGN), since x264 reads the padding beside the picture and writes the
rows and columns that round it up to whole macroblocks and one more of each. A picture is taken in
place only where every frame is encoded in the call it is passed to (sliced threads or one thread,
no lookahead delay, progressive, no OpenCL) and its strides and alignment are the layout's; it is
copied otherwise. A frame records the input frame its planes hold (i_planes_frame) apart from the
one it was given (i_input_frame), so the changed-macroblock copy brings its own planes up from
what they last held once it is copied into again, while the lowres filter and the lookahead costs
keep following the input.

Every stream comes out bit for bit as the copied picture codes it, the version SEI aside: I420
on the library before and after the change, and I420, NV12 copied, NV12 in place, in place mixed
with copied frames, and in place across releases, at CRF on one and four threads and VBV on one,
1080p and 2560x1600, a scroll, a moved window, a small change, a still screen, forced IDRs, and
frames without mb_info; 4:4:4 and 10-bit likewise. VBV on one thread compares equal built without
-ffast-math; with it, any change to x264_t's layout, an unused field alone, moves ratecontrol.c's
floating point and with it that stream. With four sliced threads at CRF 25, a frame of a
full-screen scroll at 2560x1600 (half its macroblocks changed) took 5.14 ms where it took 5.78,
and at 1080p 3.12 where it took 3.52; a 256x256 change is unchanged.
